# Cursor Rules · Shifumi Constraint Puzzle

## Game Overview
- Goal: deduce a predetermined sequence of moves (rock, paper, scissor) that yields a displayed target score (e.g., 2 wins, 2 ties, 1 loss) against a hidden computer itinerary; “erase” simply removes the most recent box in the current row prior to submission.
- Row length varies by level (3–10 boxes). Some constraint events can append extra boxes mid-run, expanding the active row length.
- Player input UI mirrors Wordle: each row has the same number of boxes as the sequence length; the target score for the round is shown above the grid.
- Moves are locked in across a row before submission; each new attempt spawns a fresh row.

## Resolution & Feedback
- After a row is submitted, compare each player move to the matching computer move.
- Color feedback per box reflects the duel outcome for that slot:
  - Green: player wins the encounter.
  - Yellow: encounter is a tie (e.g., paper vs paper).
  - Red: player loses or the slot violates a hard constraint.
- Aggregate score (wins, ties, losses) is tallied per row to determine if the attempt fulfills the displayed target.

## Constraints & Progression
- Baseline state: only the target score guides the player.
- As time elapses (every X seconds), overlay extra constraints such as “Box 3 must be a win,” “Final box must be rock,” or “Add another box.”
- Constraints stack and remain active until the game is solved. Designers may replace an older constraint with a new one, but nothing expires automatically.
- Violating a constraint should immediately flag the relevant box (e.g., outline/error marker) before submission when possible.

## Win Condition
- Player wins once an entered combination simultaneously satisfies all active constraints and matches the exact target score distribution.
- Optional systems such as attempt caps, timers, or score multipliers can layer on top of this core loop.

## Implementation Notes
- Keep the UI tap-friendly for phones; defer multi-touch gestures.
- Consider background generation of the hidden computer itinerary plus constraint schedule per level so runs are deterministic and testable.
- Provide hooks for telemetry (e.g., attempts taken, constraint breaches) to tune future puzzle difficulty.
